Subject: A dead cache site stays dead, a swap of two ways of a site moves no epoch, and the epoch's movers can be counted

What this does
  - `regvm_pic_install` returns at once for a site that is dead (empty with the miss limit reached). The contract stated above `REG_PIC_MISS_LIMIT` is that such a site "stays empty",
    but the next fill installed into it again, and the fill after that dropped it again at the limit: a polymorphic site cycled between cached and empty and moved
    `aot_pic_epoch` twice (install and entry reset) per fill, 3 times counting the install's own bump. A site that is dead is served by the global stub cache.
  - The secondary-way promotion in `regvm_get_field_ic_x` and `regvm_get_field_ic_slow` (at 16 hits the primary and the secondary way swap) no longer moves the epoch. A swap
    changes which of two shapes of one site sits in the first way; it changes no fact about a shape or about a prototype, and the entry statics, the group shapes and the method
    tables trust shapes, not the order of the ways.
  - `AOT_EPOCH_BUMP(rt, id)` replaces each of the 12 `aot_pic_epoch++` statements. In a build with `-DQJS_AOT_BUMPSTATS=1` it counts each mover, counts shape frees and in-place edits
    (also of shapes a cell ever held, marked by `JSShape.aot_observed`), keeps a table of the sites that install most, and prints them with `QJS_AOT_STATS`. Otherwise it is the bare increment.

Why we need it
  Measured in steady state, per unit: the epoch moved about 17.5k times on both raytrace and deltablue, and only about 250 of those were anything about a prototype or a layout; the rest were
  cell installs, entry resets and way swaps from sites that never settled. Every body that trusts the epoch left at the next safepoint after each move and refilled its statics. With this
  patch the epoch does not move at all in steady state on either kernel, and raytrace C.Minstr falls 3.4% (1327.6 -> 1282.8), deltablue 1.9% (806.0 -> 790.5).

Soundness
  - A dead site that no longer installs is served by the same path it was served by between installs: the global stub cache, which validates by shape pointer and `shape_free_epoch`, and the
    engine lookup. No consumer can rely on a dead site's cell: the entry checks refuse an empty hot cell (softly) and a cold site tests its own cell.
  - The swap bump: every consumer of the epoch that reads a cell at fill time learns shapes and slots, which stay true after a swap (the shape is still in a way); the consumer that reads cells at run
    time after validation (`qjs_aot_ctor_replayable`, add-transition cells) is unaffected, since a swap happens only on a read site's own/proto kinds. An entry check that wants way 0 to hold the group's shape
    still sees the swapped order and refuses, as before.
  - The counters change no behaviour.

Behaviour changes
  The interpreter no longer re-caches a site that died; such a site reads through the global stub cache or the engine lookup every time. Interpreter instructions on the kit: see the report (deltablue +1.1%).

Functions touched
  `regvm_pic_install`, `regvm_get_field_ic_x`, `regvm_get_field_ic_slow`, the 12 sites of `aot_pic_epoch++`, `js_free_shape0`, `js_shape_prepare_update`, `js_aot_stats` printing.

No.

